Step 1. Prepare Your Application
Create a Virtual Environment (Optional but Recommended):
In your project folder, run:

bash
Copy
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
Create requirements.txt:
List your Python dependencies. For example:

nginx
Copy
streamlit
boto3
pandas
plotly
Create Your Dockerfile:
Place this file in the root of your project (the same folder as your app.py). For example:

dockerfile
Copy

# Use an official Python runtime as a base image

FROM python:3.11-slim

# Update system packages

RUN apt-get update && apt-get upgrade -y && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/\*

# Set the working directory in the container

WORKDIR /app

# Copy the requirements file into the container

COPY requirements.txt .

# Install dependencies

RUN pip install --upgrade pip && pip install -r requirements.txt

# Copy the rest of your application code

COPY . .

# Expose port 8080 (Elastic Beanstalk’s default for containerized web apps)

EXPOSE 8080

# Run the Streamlit app with CORS disabled and on port 8080

CMD ["streamlit", "run", "app.py", "--server.port=8080", "--server.enableCORS=false"]
Verify Your App Locally with Docker (Optional):
Build your Docker image and run it locally to ensure everything works:

bash
Copy
docker build -t my-streamlit-app .
docker run -p 8080:8080 my-streamlit-app
Then, open your browser at http://localhost:8080 to test.

Step 2. Configure Elastic Beanstalk (EB CLI Setup)
Install the EB CLI:
For macOS:

bash
Copy
brew install awsebcli
For other platforms, follow the AWS EB CLI installation guide.

Initialize Your EB Application:
In your project directory, run:

bash
Copy
eb init
When prompted for the application name, use (for example) energyDashboard.

Select your AWS region (e.g., ca-central-1).

Choose Docker as your platform.

Complete the prompts (you can skip CodeCommit and choose SSH if needed for debugging).

Confirm Environment Permissions:
Make sure your IAM user and the Elastic Beanstalk EC2 instance role have the necessary permissions, especially:

For Elastic Beanstalk actions (e.g., CreateApplication, CreateEnvironment, etc.)

For EC2 actions (e.g., ec2:GetSecurityGroupsForVpc)

For S3 access on your specific bucket (s3:GetObject on arn:aws:s3:::dana-minicapstone-ca/incoming/_ and /predictions/_)

Step 3. Create and Deploy the Environment
Create the Environment:
Run the following command. (Since EB shows the platform as “docker”, simply specify that.)

bash
Copy
eb create my-streamlit-env --platform docker
If an environment with this name already exists and you want to update it instead, use:

bash
Copy
eb deploy my-streamlit-env
Monitor the Deployment:
The EB CLI will upload your source bundle and attempt to create the environment. You’ll see status messages similar to:

Creating security groups

Configuring load balancers, auto scaling, etc.

Deployments errors will be logged (if any, check with eb logs).

Wait for Completion:
When the deployment process finishes successfully, you will see details including the environment's CNAME (public URL). For example:

makefile
Copy
CNAME: my-streamlit-env.eba-XXXXXX.ca-central-1.elasticbeanstalk.com
Use this URL to access your application.

Step 4. Verify and Monitor Your Application
Open the Public URL:
Navigate in your browser to:

arduino
Copy
http://my-streamlit-env.eba-XXXXXX.ca-central-1.elasticbeanstalk.com/
Your Streamlit dashboard should load without errors.

Troubleshooting:
If you see errors (for example, S3 access issues), verify your IAM policies:

Confirm the instance role (e.g., aws-elasticbeanstalk-ec2-role) has a policy granting S3 GetObject on dana-minicapstone-ca/incoming/_ and /predictions/_.

Update the policy if needed and then redeploy your application if corrections are made.

Logging:
Use:

bash
Copy
eb logs
to retrieve logs from your environment for further debugging if required.
